  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "The values of \\(y\\) which will satisfy the equations\n\n\\[ \\begin{aligned} 2x^2+6x+5y+1&=0,\\\\ 2x+y+3&=0 \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nmay be found by solving:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(y^2+14y-7=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(y^2+8y+1=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(y^2+10y-7=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(y^2+y-12=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "None of the above equations"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1950-p6.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "From the second equation, \\(x=-\\tfrac{y+3}{2}.\\) Substituting into the first equation and multiplying by \\(2\\) gives \\[ \\begin{aligned} &(y+3)^2-6(y+3)\\\\ &\\quad+10y+2=0,\\\\ &y^2+10y-7=0. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "Solve the linear equation for \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\)",
        "Substitute \\(x=-\\frac{(y+3)}{2}\\) into the first equation and clear the denominator"
      ],
